The mail of a Debian system is /var/mail (Debian Policy 11.6: the mail spool is
2775 root:mail, so a person can empty a mailbox but not delete it). A queue of a
mail program that is no longer installed is listed below only when dpkg says
its packages are gone and its program is not on the system. The part between
the QUEUES markers is written by sparky-stereo-housekeeping, which writes this
file again whenever a mail program is installed or removed.

-->
<cleaner id="system_mail" os="linux">
  <label>System mail</label>
  <description>Mail the computer sent to itself and left on the disk</description>
  <option id="dead_letter">
    <label>Dead letters</label>
    <description>Deletes dead.letter in your home folder, the copy a mail program keeps of a message it could not send, such as the alert of a failed security check. As administrator it deletes root's</description>
    <action command="delete" search="file" path="~/dead.letter"/>
  </option>
  <option id="mailbox">
    <label>Local mailbox</label>
    <description>Empties your own mailbox in /var/mail. It holds what the system mailed to you: notices from cron, sudo, security and disk checks, and replies to mail the system could not deliver</description>
    <warning>This empties the whole local mailbox, so mail that people sent to this computer's local account is deleted too. Mail on a mail server, or in the folders of Thunderbird, KMail or Evolution, is not touched.</warning>
    <action command="truncate" search="file" path="/var/mail/$USER"/>
  </option>
  <option id="mailboxes">
    <label>All local mailboxes (administrator)</label>
    <description>Empties every mailbox in /var/mail and the saved mail of root (/root/mbox). Run it as administrator</description>
    <warning>This empties the local mailbox of every account, so mail that people sent to any local account is deleted too. Mail on a mail server, or in the folders of Thunderbird, KMail or Evolution, is not touched.</warning>
    <action command="truncate" search="walk.files" path="/var/mail" nregex="\.lock$"/>
    <action command="truncate" search="file" path="/root/mbox"/>
  </option>
  <option id="queues">
    <label>Queues of removed mail programs</label>
    <description>Deletes the messages still waiting in the queue of a mail program that is no longer installed (exim4, postfix, nullmailer, dma, msmtp). The queue of a mail program that is installed is never touched. Run it as administrator, except for msmtp, whose queue is in your home folder</description>
    <warning>Messages that never left the computer are deleted. They cannot be sent any more, because their mail program is gone.</warning>
<!-- QUEUES -->
<!-- /QUEUES -->
  </option>
</cleaner>
